  "title": "API Gateway Fundamentals",
  "description": "Understand the API Gateway pattern and its role in microservices.",
  "order": 1,
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedMinutes": 16,
  "tags": ["api-gateway", "fundamentals"],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "An API Gateway is a single entry point for all clients. It routes requests to services, handles cross-cutting concerns, and can aggregate responses."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Key Concepts"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "key-terms",
      "data": {
        "terms": [
          {
            "term": "API Gateway",
            "definition": "A server that is the single entry point into the system."
          },
          {
            "term": "Reverse Proxy",
            "definition": "A server that forwards requests to backend services."
          },
          {
            "term": "Edge Gateway",
            "definition": "A gateway at the edge of the network, handling external traffic."
          },
          {
            "term": "Cross-Cutting Concern",
            "definition": "A concern that spans multiple services (auth, logging, rate limiting)."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Gateway Responsibilities"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "Request routing to services",
          "Authentication and authorization",
          "Rate limiting and throttling",
          "Response aggregation",
          "Protocol translation",
          "Logging and monitoring"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Gateway vs Reverse Proxy"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "data": {
        "headers": ["Aspect", "Reverse Proxy", "API Gateway"],
        "rows": [
          ["Routing", "Path-based", "Service-aware"],
          ["Auth", "Limited", "Built-in"],
          ["Aggregation", "No", "Yes"],
          ["Rate Limiting", "Basic", "Advanced"],
          ["Protocol", "HTTP only", "HTTP, gRPC, WebSocket"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Benefits"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "Single entry point simplifies clients",
          "Centralized cross-cutting concerns",
          "Reduced chattiness between client and services",
          "Easier to monitor and secure"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Risks"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "Single point of failure",
          "Can become a bottleneck",
          "Risk of becoming a monolith",
          "Added latency"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Architecture"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "mermaid",
      "data": {
